<p>Roughly speaking, the Loongson transcodes a simple ASCII file (the README.md file) at 10 GB/s whereas the Intel processor does it slightly faster than 20 GB/s.</p>
<p>Overall, I find these results quite good for the Loongson processor.</p>
<p><a href="https://chipsandcheese.com/p/loongson-3a6000-a-star-among-chinese-cpus">The folks at Chips and Cheese have a more extensive review</a>. They put the Chinese processor somewhere between the first AMD Zen processors and the AMD Zen 2 processors on a per core basis. The AMD Zen 2 processors power current gaming consoles such as the PlayStation 5. Chips and Cheese concluded &#8220;Engineers at Loongson have a lot to be proud of&#8221;: I agree.</p>
